---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Architecture — Actual Budget Tweaks"
	description="How features, the runtime, and the Actual API bridge fit together — for anyone contributing a new feature."
>
	<main class="docs center">
		<header class="docs__header stack stack--sm">
			<a href="/" class="docs__back">&larr; Back to home</a>
			<h1>Architecture</h1>
			<p class="docs__subtitle">
				How this extension is put together, for anyone about to add a feature to it. If you're
				looking for a step-by-step walkthrough instead, see <a href="/docs/contributing">Contributing</a
				>.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The shape of a feature</h2>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li>One call to <code>defineSetting()</code>, exported as a named const</li>
				<li>Listed in <code>src/features/index.ts</code> — that's the whole registration step</li>
				<li>No plugin manifest, no separate registry file</li>
			</ul>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`// src/features/readability/category-progress/index.ts
export const categoryProgress = defineSetting({
  type: "checkbox",
  label: "Category Progress Bars",
  description: "Show a spending progress bar under each budget category.",
  group: "Budget",
  icon: "gauge",
  context: { key: "category-progress-indicators", defaultValue: false },
  css: () => CSS,
  init: () => {
    const unwatch = watchDom(scanAndDecorate);
    return () => {
      unwatch();
      undecorateAll();
    };
  },
});`}</code></pre>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li><code>defineSetting()</code> does nothing at runtime — it's the identity function</li>
				<li>Its only job is inferring a precise type from what you pass it</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The four setting types</h2>
			<div class="docs__cards">
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">checkbox</code>
					<p>Single on/off toggle. Most tweaks are this.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">select</code>
					<p>Dropdown of named options (<code>options: [&#123; value, label &#125;]</code>).</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">custom</code>
					<p>
						Own Svelte component instead of a checkbox/select row. No cleanup return on
						<code>init</code> — manages its own lifecycle.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="docs__card">
					<code class="docs__card-type">core</code>
					<p>No UI card, always active. Extension internals only — a new feature won't need this.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Runtime lifecycle</h2>
			<p><code>src/features/runtime.ts</code> is the only place that calls a setting's init/cleanup.</p>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li>
					<code>bootstrapSettings()</code> activates every enabled setting concurrently
					(<code>Promise.all</code>) — one slow feature doesn't delay another's CSS
				</li>
				<li>
					Toggling a setting calls <code>applySettingChange()</code>: deactivate old value, activate
					new one
				</li>
				<li>
					Every init/cleanup/bootstrap call is wrapped in try/catch and logged on failure — <strong
						>a throwing feature doesn't take others down with it</strong
					>, no need for your own top-level try/catch
				</li>
				<li>
					What it <em>doesn't</em> do: cleanup correctness. Whatever <code>init</code> sets up, only your
					returned cleanup undoes it
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2 id="page-gating">Page gating</h2>
			<p>
				Actual is a single-page app — gate inside a permanent <code>watchDom()</code> callback, not
				with start/stop on route change.
			</p>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`import { watchDom } from "@lib/utilities/dom-watcher";
import { Page, matchesPage } from "@lib/utilities/pages";

function scanAndDecorate() {
  if (!matchesPage(Page.Budget)) return;
  // ...decorate whatever's currently in the DOM
}

// registered once in init(), left running for the setting's whole lifetime
const unwatch = watchDom(scanAndDecorate);`}</code></pre>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li><code>watchDom()</code> is a ref-counted wrapper around a debounced MutationObserver</li>
				<li>No <code>target</code> passed → shares one observer on <code>document.body</code></li>
				<li>
					<code>target</code> passed → that node gets its own observer, deduped by target — cheap to
					add another listener
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>The Actual API bridge</h2>
			<p>
				<code>src/lib/utilities/actual-api.ts</code> exports <code>query()</code>, <code>send()</code
				>, <code>dispatch()</code>, <code>navigate()</code> — typed wrappers around Actual's internal
				<code>$q</code>/<code>$query</code>/<code>$send</code> and action-creators, reached via a
				bridge injected into the page's main JS world.
			</p>
			<pre class="docs__code"><code>{`const cats = await query("categories");
const cell = await send("get-cell", { sheetName: "budget202506", name: "available-funds" });`}</code></pre>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li><strong>The bridge itself is generic</strong> — <code>query</code>/<code>send</code> forward whatever table/method name you give them</li>
				<li>Typed autocomplete comes from <code>ActualTable</code>/<code>SendMethodMap</code> in <code>src/lib/types/actual-schema.ts</code></li>
				<li>Need an untyped table/method? Usually a type-only change there — the runtime bridge doesn't need to change</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Storage</h2>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li>
					<code>src/lib/utilities/store.ts</code> exports <code>getValue</code>/<code>setValue</code
					>/<code>hasValue</code> — thin wrappers around <code>browser.storage.local</code>,
					<code>local:</code>-prefixed keys
				</li>
				<li>A setting's own value (<code>context.key</code>) is already handled by the runtime</li>
				<li>Reach for these directly only for extra state (cached data, per-item settings)</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Registering a feature</h2>
			<p><code>pnpm new-feature</code> does this for you — see <a href="/docs/contributing">Contributing</a>. By hand:</p>
			<ul class="docs__list">
				<li>Add the import to <code>src/features/index.ts</code></li>
				<li>
					Add the identifier to its category array (<code>readability</code>,
					<code>appearance</code>, <code>layoutAndDensity</code>, <code>workflows</code>) — one array,
					feeds both the settings panel and the public <a href="/features">features list</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>label</code>/<code>description</code>/<code>icon</code>/<code>group</code> must be
					plain string literals — the features-list generator reads them via AST parsing, and a
					computed value comes back empty with no error
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</main>
</Base>

<style>
	.docs {
		padding-block: 3rem 5rem;
		max-inline-size: 760px;
	}

	.docs__header h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
	}

	.docs__subtitle {
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		font-size: 1rem;
		max-inline-size: 60ch;
	}

	.docs__back {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.docs__back:hover {
		color: var(--accent);
	}

	.docs__section {
		margin-block-start: 3rem;
	}

	.docs__section h2 {
		font-size: 1.3rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: -0.02em;
		margin-block-end: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__section p {
		font-size: 0.92rem;
		line-height: 1.65;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.docs__section p + p {
		margin-block-start: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__section code {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.82em;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
	}

	.docs__section strong {
		color: var(--text);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.docs__code {
		margin-block: 1rem;
		padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
		background: var(--dark-bg);
		border: 1px solid var(--dark-border);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
		overflow-x: auto;
	}

	.docs__code code {
		background: none;
		color: var(--dark-text-light);
		padding: 0;
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
		white-space: pre;
	}

	.docs__list {
		list-style: none;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin-block: 1rem;
	}

	.docs__list li {
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
		padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
		position: relative;
	}

	.docs__list li::before {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		inset-inline-start: 0;
		inset-block-start: 0.5rem;
		inline-size: 5px;
		block-size: 5px;
		border-radius: 50%;
		background: var(--accent);
	}

	.docs__cards {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 0.9rem;
	}

	.docs__card {
		padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
		background: var(--surface);
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
	}

	.docs__card-type {
		display: inline-block;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
		margin-block-end: 0.5rem;
	}

	.docs__card p {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.55;
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.docs__card code {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 0.85em;
		background: var(--accent-wash);
		color: var(--accent);
		padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
		border-radius: 3px;
	}

	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.docs__cards {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}
	}
</style>
